The Singleton Design Pattern is a creational design pattern that ensures:

Only one instance of a class can exist.
The instance is globally accessible throughout the application.

Why use Singleton?
You use a singleton when exactly one object should coordinate actions across the system, such as:

Database connections
Configuration managers
Logging services
Cache managers

Advantages

✅ Controlled access to a single instance
✅ Saves memory/resources when only one object is needed
✅ Provides a global access point

Disadvantages

❌ Acts like a global variable, increasing coupling
❌ Harder to unit test and mock
❌ Can cause concurrency issues if not implemented correctly